{
  "case_id": "2006-06-562",
  "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 06-562, United States versus Atlantic Research Corporation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondent seeks to circumvent the limitations that Congress imposed on that remedy by manufacturing a parallel mechanism for contribution-like relief under Section 107. Respondent's theory should be rejected because it violates fundamental canons of statutory construction, renders Section 113(f) superfluous, makes a mockery of the textual limitations enforced by this Court in Cooper Industries, and would frustrate Congress's clear intent to encourage settlements with the government in order to achieve supervised effective cleanups."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Section 113(f) of CERCLA, Congress created a precisely drawn detailed mechanism for potentially responsible parties to recover their response costs from other PRPs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Hungar, was this Court wrong in your view, it wasn't dispositive of the case in Key Tronic when it said, \"Section 107 unquestionably provides a cause of action for private parties to seek recovery of cleanup costs?\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, the sky will fall because under that interpretation PRPs can evade the settlement bar that Congress enacted in order to encourage settlement, and Congress's clear goal in providing a contribution remedy and providing an explicit opportunity for PRPs to sue was to encourage settlement with the Government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Can I -- can I go back?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. HUNGAR",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "EPA has entered into settlements with literally tens of thousands of PRPs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But if Respondent has a cost recovery claim under Section 107, the settlement bar goes out the window."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF OWEN T. ARMSTRONG, JR. ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What about the disincentive to settlement? You just said how important settlement was, or what a good thing it is to encourage it. But the Government says if you could just clean up without any order and without making any proposed settlement, just do it and get back your costs, then there's a powerful disincentive to settle."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So you say there are two factors which still exert pressure to settle. One is that you don't have to bear the burden of showing that you conform with the national contingency plan, and second, that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Do you rely at all on -- in 9607(a)(b)? It says any other necessary costs of response incurred by any other person. Does the word \"any\" add anything to your case? Do you think it urges us to give the broadest possible interpretation to other person?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So that part of your case is that, is to say that it's limited by 113 is to say that there's been an implied repeal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask just one question about your position on the remedial settlement provision in 113(f)(2)? Do you read it to provide not only immunity from contribution suits but from any other litigation whatsoever?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jay D. Geck (Amicus)",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GECK: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. May it please the Court: There are more than 400,000 sites across the country that are contaminated by hazardous wastes. The amici States recognize that if these sites are to be cleaned up, it's going to take the work of private parties."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why wouldn't the States prefer to proceed, to force people to proceed under 113, so that the States would have, you know, a hand in deciding how the cleanup should go? I don't -- I don't quite understand why you're here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-562",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a043c179163206a321412be56291dbd9ca6ef39bc91fa2b495ec0f770b6939e7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a043c1791632-0003-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Hungar. The case is submitted.\n(Whereupon, at 11:02 a.m., the case in the\nabove-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
    {
